<DocsLayout title="Client assurance (attestation)">
  <h1>Client assurance (attestation)</h1>
  <p>
    Client assurance proves what a <em>client</em> is — a genuine, unmodified
    build of your app on genuine hardware (iOS App Attest, Android Play
    Integrity) or a human-passed web client (Cloudflare Turnstile, Google
    reCAPTCHA v3) — independently of whether anyone is signed in. Verified
    evidence is exchanged for a short-lived <strong>assurance token</strong>
    that clients attach as the <code>X-Assurance-Token</code> header. It is
    the equivalent of Firebase App Check.
  </p>
  <p>
    The token is a JWT signed by the deployment's JWKS with
    <code>aud: "assurance"</code>, an <code>amr</code> claim naming the
    provider that passed, the minting project, and — deliberately — no
    subject. It can never authenticate as a user, and a user token can never
    pass as assurance. Downstream services can require it on their own APIs
    by validating against <code>/.well-known/jwks.json</code> and checking
    the audience.
  </p>

  <h2>Flow</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>iOS</strong> — <code>CreateAssuranceChallenge</code>
      (platform <code>ios</code>) → generate a key with
      <code>DCAppAttestService</code>, attest over the challenge string's
      UTF-8 bytes → <code>IssueAssuranceToken</code> with the key id +
      attestation object. The attested key is registered server-side. To
      renew, sign a fresh challenge with the same key and call
      <code>RefreshAssuranceToken</code> — the hardware counter provides
      replay protection, so there is no stored refresh secret to steal.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Android</strong> — <code>CreateAssuranceChallenge</code>
      (platform <code>android</code>) → request a Play Integrity token
      passing the challenge string <em>verbatim</em> as the nonce (Play
      echoes it back, and the server compares it as a string) →
      <code>IssueAssuranceToken</code> with the integrity token. The verdict
      is decoded server-side via Google (never locally). Renewal repeats the
      same exchange.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Web</strong> — solve the captcha, then
      <code>IssueAssuranceToken</code> with platform <code>web</code> and
      the solution. No challenge round-trip; the provider runs its own.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Enforcement</h2>
  <p>
    When assurance is enabled, six unauthenticated endpoints require a valid
    assurance token, each independently switchable (all default on):
    <code>PasswordSignup</code>, <code>PasswordLogin</code>,
    <code>RequestPasswordReset</code>, <code>RequestEmailLoginCode</code>,
    <code>RequestMagicLink</code>, and <code>BeginPasskeySignup</code>.
    The token is reusable until it expires — verification is a local JWKS
    check, no provider round-trip per request.
  </p>

  <h2>Configuration</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>GATEWAY_ASSURANCE_ENABLED</code> (default <code>false</code>) —
      global switch for the whole surface. Off: the three RPCs return
      Unimplemented and nothing is gated. On, the server refuses to boot
      unless at least one arm (web provider, iOS app, or Android app) is
      configured — otherwise the enforce toggles would deny every auth
      endpoint with no way to obtain a token.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>GATEWAY_ASSURANCE_WEB_PROVIDER</code> —
      <code>turnstile</code> or <code>recaptcha_v3</code>, with
      <code>GATEWAY_ASSURANCE_TURNSTILE_SECRET</code> /
      <code>GATEWAY_ASSURANCE_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY</code> or
      <code>GATEWAY_ASSURANCE_RECAPTCHA_SECRET</code> /
      <code>GATEWAY_ASSURANCE_RECAPTCHA_SCORE_THRESHOLD</code>. Empty
      disables web assurance (mobile-attestation-only deployment).
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>GATEWAY_ASSURANCE_IOS_TEAM_ID</code> +
      <code>GATEWAY_ASSURANCE_IOS_BUNDLE_ID</code> (+ optional
      <code>GATEWAY_ASSURANCE_IOS_ENV</code>, <code>production</code> |
      <code>development</code>) — the default project's App Attest app
      identity.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>GATEWAY_ASSURANCE_ANDROID_PACKAGE_NAME</code> +
      <code>GATEWAY_ASSURANCE_ANDROID_CERT_SHA256_DIGESTS</code>
      (comma-separated, base64url) +
      <code>GATEWAY_ASSURANCE_ANDROID_SA_KEY_JSON</code> (Google
      service-account key) — the default project's Play Integrity identity.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>GATEWAY_ASSURANCE_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS</code> (default 3600, max
      86400) and <code>GATEWAY_ASSURANCE_CHALLENGE_TTL_SECONDS</code>
      (default 300, max 3600). An assurance token cannot be revoked, so the
      lifetime is capped; a web token is reusable for its whole TTL, so
      shorten it if you want closer to per-request captcha assurance.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_ASSURANCE_PER_IP</code> (default 20) —
      per-IP quota per rate-limit window on all three assurance RPCs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>GATEWAY_ASSURANCE_ALLOW_PROJECT_ONLY</code> (default
      <code>false</code>) — boot with assurance enabled and no env arm,
      for deployments where every app identity lives in per-project
      <code>config_json</code>. Not needed on Postgres (a control plane can
      carry that config, so it is assumed); required to opt in on drivers
      without one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>GATEWAY_ASSURANCE_ENFORCE_PASSWORD_SIGNUP</code>,
      <code>…_PASSWORD_LOGIN</code>, <code>…_PASSWORD_RESET</code>,
      <code>…_EMAIL_LOGIN_CODE</code>, <code>…_MAGIC_LINK</code>,
      <code>…_PASSKEY_SIGNUP</code> (all default <code>true</code>) —
      per-endpoint gates, active only while assurance is enabled.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Per-project app identities live in the project's <code>config_json</code>
    under <code>assurance</code>. Author them with the operator RPC
    <code>AdminSetProjectAssurance</code>, which takes the Play Integrity
    service-account key in <strong>plaintext</strong> and encrypts it
    server-side under <code>GATEWAY_PROJECT_SECRETS_KEY</code> — exactly like
    <code>AdminSetProjectOAuthProvider</code> handles an OAuth client secret.
    Reads never echo the key back, only whether one is stored. A project's own
    block always wins; the env identity applies to the default project when it
    configures none.
  </p>
  <Code
    lang="bash"
    code={`curl -s https://id.example.com/identity.v1.IdentityService/AdminSetProjectAssurance \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -H "X-Admin-Secret: $GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET" \
  -d '{
        "projectId": "proj_123",
        "config": {
          "iosTeamId": "ABCDE12345",
          "iosBundleId": "com.example.app",
          "androidPackageName": "com.example.app",
          "androidCertSha256Digests": ["<base64url-sha256>"],
          "androidServiceAccountKey": "{\"client_email\":\"…\",\"private_key\":\"…\"}"
        }
      }'
# → {"config":{…,"hasServiceAccountKey":true}}   (the key itself is never returned)`}
  />

  <h2>Example: web exchange</h2>
  <Code
    lang="bash"
    code={`# 1. Exchange a captcha solution for an assurance token
curl -s https://id.example.com/identity.v1.IdentityService/IssueAssuranceToken \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"platform":"web","webToken":"<captcha-solution>"}'
# → {"assuranceToken":"eyJ…","expiresAtMs":…}

# 2. Attach it to a gated endpoint
curl -s https://id.example.com/identity.v1.IdentityService/PasswordSignup \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -H 'X-Assurance-Token: eyJ…' \\
  -d '{"email":"a@example.com","password":"…"}'`}
  />

  <h2>What assurance is not</h2>
  <p>
    Attestation proves <em>genuine app on genuine hardware</em>, not
    human-rate usage — one real device driven by automation still passes.
    Its durable value is a stable, expensive-to-farm identifier (the
    attested device) for downstream rate-limiting to key on. It is also
    orthogonal to the per-project <a href="/docs/projects/access">access
    mode</a>: assurance authenticates the client, the access mode governs
    which users may authenticate. See
    <a href="https://github.com/elloloop/identity/blob/main/docs/adr/0012-client-assurance-layer.md">ADR-0012</a>
    for the design.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
